            "output": "<path>/Users/itsmeyaw/projects/alpaca-hackathon/docs/adr/0011-separate-alpaca-runtime-and-agent-tool-authority.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # ADR-0011: Separate Alpaca Runtime and Agent Tool Authority\n2: \n3: - Status: Accepted\n4: - Date: 2026-08-27\n5: \n6: ## Context\n7: \n8: The project should visibly use Alpaca's SDK, MCP server, and CLI without allowing an LLM to bypass deterministic execution controls. The always-on service also needs streaming and reconciliation behavior that is better expressed directly through the Python SDK.\n9: \n10: ## Decision\n11: \n12: Assign each Alpaca integration a distinct role:\n13: \n14: - Use `alpaca-py` for runtime market streams, trading updates, account reconciliation, historical requests, and order submission.\n15: - Give the Bedrock-hosted LLM read-oriented Alpaca MCP tools for structured research and explainable demonstrations.\n16: - Do not expose a direct MCP order tool to the LLM. Only the Risk and Execution module can translate an approved Trade Intent into an Order Plan.\n17: - Use the Alpaca CLI for operator inspection, dry runs, health checks, and the judging demonstration.\n18: \n19: ## Consequences\n20: \n21: - MCP cannot become a second, weakly governed execution path.\n22: - SDK and MCP observations need timestamps and provenance when included in Decision Records.\n23: - The demo can show all three Alpaca surfaces without misrepresenting which component owns execution.\n24: - Integration tests need fake or paper implementations at the SDK and MCP boundaries.\n\n(End of file - total 24 lines)\n</content>",
