            "output": "<path>/Users/itsmeyaw/projects/alpaca-hackathon/docs/adr/0005-keep-trained-models-in-shadow-mode.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # ADR-0005: Keep Trained Models in Shadow Mode\n2: \n3: - Status: Superseded by ADR-0013\n4: - Date: 2026-08-27\n5: \n6: ## Context\n7: \n8: Tree models may improve nonlinear return forecasts, but a short competition encourages overfitting and makes weak validation difficult to detect. The event-routing hypothesis can be expressed as deterministic, testable expert scores without making a trained model part of the execution critical path.\n9: \n10: ## Decision\n11: \n12: Use deterministic catalyst, momentum, reversion, regime, and risk scores as the Incumbent Strategy.\n13: \n14: Train LightGBM or XGBoost only as a Challenger Model. Record its predictions beside incumbent decisions, but do not let those predictions alter live Trade Intents. Promotion requires chronological walk-forward validation, purged overlapping labels, point-in-time inputs, conservative execution costs, and better results than the incumbent and simple baselines.\n15: \n16: ## Consequences\n17: \n18: - The first live system remains explainable and reproducible.\n19: - The decision schema must store incumbent and challenger outputs separately.\n20: - The dashboard can demonstrate AI/ML comparison without presenting shadow returns as traded P&L.\n21: - Promotion is a deliberate versioned decision, not an automatic response to a few competition outcomes.\n22: \n23: ADR-0013 records the competition operator's explicit decision to authorize the selected model for\n24: bounded Alpaca paper execution despite the promotion evidence remaining incomplete.\n\n(End of file - total 24 lines)\n</content>",
